From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ole Algoritme <olealgoritme@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 14:10:55 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] nvk: GPU time per render pass and command buffer behind
 NVK_PASS_PROFILE

To find what makes a frame GPU-heavy without a vendor profiler: with
NVK_PASS_PROFILE=1 every render pass and every primary graphics command
buffer gets a slot in a mapped GART ring at record time, and the 3D class
writes a timestamped report (pipeline location ALL) at its start and its
end. After submits the CPU harvests finished slots and adds end - begin to
a per-signature total. A pass's signature is its size, sample count, layer
and attachment counts and, for the first colour and the depth attachment,
the format, LINEAR or block-linear, the PTE kind and whether the image is
compressed, so the log also shows how the attachments are laid out. Every
two seconds the 24 heaviest signatures are logged with GPU ms per second,
us per pass, count and draws per pass; the command-buffer line is the
whole GPU time, so what no pass explains is compute, copies and clears.

The lines also go to a file: NVK_PASS_PROFILE_FILE if set, else on
Windows %ProgramData%\Helios\nvk-pass-<pid>.txt, falling back to
%TEMP%\nvk-pass-<pid>.txt (the HELIOS_VK_FRAMETIME rule; mesa's own log
never reaches a file on Windows).

Each report is a flush of pending writes plus a release, a little GPU time
per pass: good for finding a 10x, not for measuring 1 %.
